Step 1: Recording incidents

incidents: id, event_id, reported_by, source, category, reported_severity, title, description, location, occurred_at, reported_at, attachments

Step 2: Queuing incidents for Kafka

outbox: id, topic, message_key, payload, created_at, published_at, attempts, last_error

Step 3: Grouping incidents into alerts - Small ML models can be used to dedup/triage/classify incidents

alerts: id, event_id, classification, classifier_version, severity, status, title, summary, occurrence_count, first_seen_at, last_seen_at, acknowledged_by, acknowledged_at, resolved_by, resolved_at, resolution_notes, version, created_at, updated_at
alert_incidents: alert_id, incident_id, match_score, linked_at
alert_history: id, alert_id, version, action, changed_by, old_values, new_values, changed_at

Step 4: On-call agents store

oncall_agents: id, user_id, name, email, phone, active
oncall_shifts: id, agent_id, event_id, escalation_level, shift (time range)
alert_notifications: id, alert_id, agent_id, escalation_level, channel, status, provider_message_id, queued_at, sent_at, acknowledged_at, error

Step 5: ML resolution suggestions

resolution_suggestions: id, alert_id, model_name, model_version, suggestion, steps, confidence, rank, similar_alert_ids, evidence, status, reviewed_by, reviewed_at, feedback, created_at

Reference tables (owned by the portal)

tenants: id, name, created_at
events: id, title, venue, start_time, end_time
